WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.860
As Star Trek: The Next Generation&nbsp;
concluded its final episode in 1994,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.360
many fans were left wanting more. So why&nbsp;
was it gone before its time seemed up?

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.580
Not only had the series been a massive hit, but&nbsp;
it was more profitable than most TV shows at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.680
time. Trekkies clamored for comic books,&nbsp;
action figures, novels, and video games…

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.540
"Only you can make the life or death&nbsp;
decisions required of a Starfleet officer."

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.580
…so it seemed like a strange decision to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.400
end one of the most popular&nbsp;
and profitable shows on TV.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:35.400
All was not lost, however. As The New York Times&nbsp;
reported of the series finale in July 1994,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.800
franchises were a big deal in Hollywood, and&nbsp;
Paramount wasn't about to take its eye off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.540
prize, even after the end of the series. As&nbsp;
the adventures of Jean-Luc Picard wound down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:50.100
Star Trek: Deep Space Nine was launched,&nbsp;
with Star Trek: Voyager close on the horizon.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.900
Meanwhile, the cast of The Original Series,&nbsp;
led by William Shatner and Leonard Nimoy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:00.480
still hung on. They'd been starring in feature&nbsp;
films as recently as 1991, but they were aging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.980
and the decades-old franchise needed a&nbsp;
constant infusion of new blood to keep afloat.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:09.660
In 1994, the cast of The Next Generation was&nbsp;
still made up of the youngest established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.660
crew of the franchise. Deep Space&nbsp;
Nine was still finding its feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:18.000
and Paramount wanted to start making movies with a&nbsp;
familiar cast at the helm. That meant freeing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.080
Next Generation cast from their television&nbsp;
obligations. The feature film Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:28.080
Generations debuted in 1994, only months after&nbsp;
the end of the series, officially passing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.520
feature film torch from Shatner's James&nbsp;
Kirk to Patrick Stewart's Jean-Luc Picard.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.840
The crew of the Enterprise-D would explosively&nbsp;
destroy their starship in the same movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.220
ceremoniously ending their connection to&nbsp;
the small screen. With the new Enterprise-E,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.980
they continued to star in Star&nbsp;
Trek movies until Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.620
Nemesis in 2002. Despite occasional cameos&nbsp;
throughout the franchise, the cast of The Next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:54.960
Generation wouldn't truly reunite until almost&nbsp;
20 years later, for the series Star Trek: Picard.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.000
Another, more obscure reason for the cancellation&nbsp;
of Next Generation had to do with the way network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.500
television operated in the pre-streaming age.&nbsp;
At the time, Paramount was selling the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.700
directly to local stations, an unusual&nbsp;
move that had paid off well. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.140
the strategy couldn't last forever due to&nbsp;
airtime limitations; stations that bought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.000
the show were required to buy rights to reruns,&nbsp;
and to air each episode as many as seven times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.580
As the number of episodes grew with each season,&nbsp;
stations that purchased Next Generation would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.720
filling an increasing number of their limited&nbsp;
slots with old episodes, and there was no such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.620
thing as Netflix for them to offload all that&nbsp;
content without broadcast time restrictions.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.880
By then, profitability was on a downward&nbsp;
trend. As the series grew in size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:40.500
stations were less willing to pay top dollar&nbsp;
for new episodes. Meanwhile, the main cast of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.480
a long-running series traditionally had regular&nbsp;
pay raises built into their contracts, and Next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.100
Generation was no exception. The cast was getting&nbsp;
paid more as the show itself made less money.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:54.600
To this day, Star Trek: The Next Generation&nbsp;
remains a high water mark for many Star Trek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.320
fans. It's enjoying a healthy second&nbsp;
life on streaming and in syndication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.220
where it continues to be appreciated&nbsp;
by those whose dreams boldly go where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.240
no one has gone before. And, although it&nbsp;
concluded with some unresolved plotlines…

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.010
"My name is Barash."

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.320
…many would be resolved in Star Trek: Picard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.320
and most fans agree that the&nbsp;
series ended at the right time.

